packages/opencorvus/src\prompt\core\build-core.txt:112:- Never deliver a browser/UI/webpage project without a root `package.json` that declares both `packageManager` and `scripts.dev`, plus the matching committed lockfile for that package manager (`package-lock.json`, `bun.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, or `yarn.lock`). The build session must prove the real app can be served through that explicit dev script; a static `index.html` plus tests is not complete. The script must serve the real page over loopback HTTP from the repository root, and your verification must actually run it long enough to see the emitted local URL before you report success.
packages/opencorvus/src\prompt\core\build-core.txt:114:- Never inline base64-encoded binary assets into emitted source code or artifacts. Staged attachments live at `<worktree>/references/<filename>`; reference them by relative path (`<img src="references/foo.png">`, `<image href="references/foo.png">`, `./references/foo.png` for file reads, etc.) and copy them into the deliverable's asset directory through a real file step when the runtime layout demands it. `data:<mime>;base64,...` URLs, hex-encoded blobs, or PowerShell `$bytes = [Convert]::FromBase64String(...)` heredocs embedded in generated SVG/HTML/JSON/scripts are forbidden in every form — the session write-path rejects these payloads via `InlineBase64InPartError` and downstream verification will follow.
